WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.956
A phobia is a strong, lasting fear of one thing. Freud believed such fears come from hidden thoughts in the unconscious, the part of the mind we cannot see. The Little Hans case study is Freud's report on one boy with a phobia.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:37.822
In nineteen oh nine, Freud wrote about a boy called Hans, five years old when the case closed. Most facts came from letters by his father, because Freud met Hans only briefly. Hans feared horses, especially horses with blinkers and black around the mouth, and horses falling down.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:01:00.294
Freud said Hans wanted his mother to himself, and feared his father as a rival. This is the Oedipus complex. The fear moved onto horses. This is displacement, which means shifting a feeling onto a safer target. Blinkers looked like his father's glasses, and black round the mouth like a moustache.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:20.212
Hans also had dreams about losing his mother. The case is rich in detail. But it is one boy, told through his father, and Freud's meaning is only his opinion. A simpler idea is that Hans saw a horse fall in the street, and learned to fear horses by association.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:32.854
A question gives you a person and asks how well Little Hans explains their fear or dreams. A nine-mark assess question needs the case, a match, and an evaluation ending in a judgement.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:51.331
Step one: recall the case. Step two: match each detail to the person in the question. Step three: evaluate. Give similarities, then a simpler explanation, then the single case. Finish with a judgement.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:02:03.291
Here is the plan for a nine-mark answer. One paragraph for each skill, and a last line for your judgement. The trap is describing Little Hans and never mentioning the person.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:24.212
Here is a real exam question. Amy dreams her parents have left her in a shop. Barbara thinks the dream shows Amy's fear of living alone. We explain two ways Little Hans supports Barbara. Four marks.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:37.177
Way one. Hans had dreams about losing his mother, and Freud used them to show his real fear. So Amy's dream of being left could show her real fear of being alone.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:52.099
Way two. Freud said a fear can appear as a symbol. Hans's fear of his father appeared as a fear of horses. So Amy's fear of living alone may appear as a dream about a shop.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:03:03.796
A tempting wrong route is to retell the case, horses and blinkers, and stop. That is knowledge, but it does not support Barbara. The question says support, so link to Amy.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.210
The exam answer. Two ways, each with a point and a link.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:16.699
One mark for each Little Hans point and one for each link to Amy. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:30.270
The most common mistake is a wrong detail. Hans was afraid of horses, not dogs. And Freud said the fear was displaced from his father, not learned.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:43.706
Now you try a short recall question about the case. Read all four options before you choose. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:04:01.720
The answer is D, five years old. Hans was five when Freud's case study closed. Remember the other key facts too. He feared horses, and most of what Freud knew came from letters written by his father.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:14.516
A common wrong answer is C, four years old. Hans was still four when his fear of horses began. But the question asks for his age at the end of the case study, so C loses the mark.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:23.940
So recall the case exactly, match it to the person, then weigh it up and judge. In a long answer, always finish with a clear judgement.
